Workload-affine DPA packet processor
====================================

Design goal
-----------

The DPA worker must not switch packet workloads while polling. Each worker is
built with one workload and continuously polls two fixed queue lanes. Tenants
share those lanes, but the queue count does not grow with the tenant count.

The implementation supports up to 128 tenants. The default topology is four
tenants, eight workers per scheduler, two queues per worker, and one shard per
worker for each tenant.

Dispatch mapping
----------------

Traffic is steered by destination MAC. For scheduler ``s``, tenant ``t`` and
shard ``h`` the MAC offset from ``DMAC`` is:

``(s * tenants + t) * tenant_shards + h``

The host maps the flow to a fixed worker and lane:

``worker = h % workers_per_scheduler``

``lane = (t + h / workers_per_scheduler) % 2``

``queue = worker * 2 + lane``

The NIC Direct Rules entry attaches ``tenant + 1`` as a flow tag while steering
the packet to a fixed queue. The DPA worker reads the tag directly from the CQE,
applies that tenant's QoS admission state, and runs its build-time-selected
workload. It does not parse or divide the packet DMAC in the hot path. A
rejected packet is consumed without producing an SQ entry, so a restricted
tenant does not block an admitted tenant sharing the lane.

``tenant_shards=workers`` gives each tenant one fixed lane on every worker.
``tenant_shards=2*workers`` lets a hot tenant use both lanes on every worker.
The latter is the bounded, explicit resource-expansion mechanism; it does not
change the workload executed by any worker.

Resource scaling
----------------

For ``W`` workers, ``T`` tenants and ``S`` shards per tenant:

* data RQ/SQ pairs per scheduler: ``2W`` (bounded, independent of ``T``)
* ingress tenant/shard bindings per scheduler: ``T*S``
* maximum queue topology: ``W <= 32`` because a scheduler has at most 64 queues
* maximum tenants: 128
* shard range: ``1..2W``

The previous ``full_ver`` design used ``W*T`` queue pairs and therefore reached
the 64-queue limit at eight tenants when ``W=8``. It also encoded tenant identity
in the selected queue. The new design moves tenant identity to NIC flow metadata
and software admission state while keeping the worker workload fixed.

Build and run
-------------

On the DPA machine:

```
source /etc/profile.d/flexio.sh
meson setup --wipe --buildtype=release build-release
ninja -C build-release
./build-release/packet_processor/host/flexio_packet_processor \
    mlx5_bond_0 1 4 8 0 16 0 8
```

Invocation:

```
flexio_packet_processor <device> [schedulers] [tenants] [workers/scheduler] \
    [scheduler EU] [worker EU] [buffer location] [shards/tenant]
```

The program accepts QoS updates on standard input. There must be one cycle
weight and one bandwidth weight for every configured tenant:

```
qos <cycle weights...> <bandwidth weights...>
```

For four tenants, admit only tenant 1:

```
qos 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0
```

Weights are relative integers in ``0..1000000``. A zero weight is a hard deny;
the scheduler keeps the tenant restricted across accounting-period rollover.
Positive weights are guaranteed accounting shares rather than rigid output
ratios. The default bucket mode is work-conserving: unused budget can be lent to
another active tenant up to its cap, so an idle or lane-limited tenant does not
waste worker capacity.

Cycle and byte accounting is sampled once per 16 admitted packets and scaled by
16. Zero-weight admission is still checked for every packet. Sampling removes
the two global atomic updates and two cycle-clock reads from 15 of every 16
packets; positive-weight metering therefore has a 16-packet sampling
granularity, while hard denial remains exact.

The workload is fixed at build time through ``PP_WORKER_WORKLOAD_TYPE`` in
``dev/flexio_pp_dev_utils.h``. Available IDs are L2 reflector (0), checksum16
(1), checksum-nrnd (2), and nof (3). ``PP_WORKLOAD_CHECKSUM_ROUNDS`` controls
the checksum-nrnd iteration count.

Validation on 2026-09-21
------------------------

Setup: BlueField DPA ``10.14.30.226``, traffic generator ``10.14.30.112``,
512-byte packets, one scheduler, eight workers, L2-reflector workload. Values
below are receive rates reported by the external DPDK traffic generator.

| Case | Queue pairs | Result |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Existing ``full_ver``, 2 tenants | 16 | 16.08-16.17 Gbps total |
| Workload-affine, 2 active tenants, 8 shards/tenant | 16 | 16.52-16.56 Gbps total |
| Tenant 0 hard-denied, tenant 1 admitted | 16 | tenant 0: 0; tenant 1: 13.00-13.01 Gbps |
| Four-tenant topology, active weights 1:3 | 16 | 6.65 and 9.57 Gbps; 16.22 Gbps total |
| 128 tenants, 8 shards/tenant | 16 | 1,024 rules started; tenant 39 passed at 13.03-13.04 Gbps while tenant 0 stayed at 0 |

All comparison binaries used Meson ``--buildtype=release`` (DPA ``-O3`` and
LTO). The earlier 31% result came from debug binaries and also included
per-packet DMAC decoding, per-packet accounting, and diagnostic traffic
counters; it is not representative of the optimized implementation.

The optimized scalable path is about 2.5% faster than the release-built
``full_ver`` baseline in the equal two-tenant comparison. The 1:3 test is not
expected to produce a rigid 1:3 throughput ratio: tenant 1 could not consume
its complete 75% cycle allocation on one lane, so bucket rollover lent the
unused share to tenant 0. This retained 16.22 Gbps aggregate throughput while
preserving zero-weight hard isolation.

Operational notes
-----------------

* Use a clean build after changing DPA headers because the custom device target
  does not reliably notice every header dependency.
* Queue count remains bounded, but flow-rule count is ``schedulers*T*S``.
* More shards improve a hot tenant's lane coverage; they do not create or switch
  worker workloads.
* A worker polls at most 64 packets from one lane before checking the other.
  This is packet-count fairness, not a workload switch: both lanes always run
  the same build-time workload.
* Diagnostic per-tenant packet counters are deliberately absent from the hot
  path. Use generator RX statistics and the optional scheduler cycle report for
  validation.
